-- §8 I4 / §3 `inst` — THE AMBIGUITY REJECT, WITH ORIGIN-CARRYING IMPLS.
--
-- WHY THIS FIXTURE EXISTS: a §3 conformance row for a genuinely ambiguous goal
-- whose instances live in a module of their own, so the interface carries a
-- `(module, name)` identity on both the goal and the impl side.  The four
-- ambiguity fixtures in `test/typecheck_error_fixtures/` are all SINGLE-FILE, so
-- every interface in them is `OriginUnresolved` on both sides and keys `TkBare`;
-- none of them can observe an identity-keyed lookup at all.
--
-- HOW THE VERDICT IS REACHED.  The undetermined-goal verdict counts the instance
-- heads that unify with the goal (`goalCandidates`, over the impl rows `IE`
-- holds for the goal's interface).  It reads no per-interface impl count, so a
-- missed keyed lookup cannot turn this reject quiet by answering zero; a miss
-- elsewhere in the `ImplUniverse` channel is a REJECT (a payload table's miss
-- means "no impl exists").
--
-- SPEC ANSWER, HAND-DERIVED, NOT CAPTURED.  `roundtrip : Mk a => Int -> Int` fixes
-- `a` in neither its argument nor its result, so at `roundtrip 14` the predicate
-- `Mk ?a` reaches §3 `inst` with `?a` free and quantified by nothing.
-- `match(IE, Mk ?a)` is {`Mk Widget`, `Mk Gadget`} — two impls, mutually
-- ⊑-incomparable (no substitution turns `Widget` into `Gadget`), so there is no
-- unique ⊑-minimum and the goal is genuinely ambiguous.  §3 requires a UNIQUE
-- selection; the implementation's deliberate divergence from the OCaml oracle's
-- silent first-wins is to REJECT (`T-AMBIGUOUS-INSTANCE`).  Both engines must
-- reject at check time, so there is no value to pin.
--
-- THIS FIXTURE DOES NOT DISCRIMINATE THE #1446 IDENTITY RE-KEY.  Every
-- identity-carrying impl is ALSO indexed under its bare spelling
-- (`insertUnivImplKeys`), so a reader that keyed the interface `TkBare` would
-- still find both impls, and the verdict would be the same.  Neutering the
-- WRITER instead produces hundreds of `No impl of` in the compiler's own closure,
-- which `check-self` catches far more loudly, so this row adds no unique coverage
-- there either.
--
-- It also behaves identically on the base binary, so it witnesses nothing about
-- #1446.  It is kept as a §3 CONFORMANCE row — the verdict must reject a genuinely
-- ambiguous cross-module goal — and NOT as evidence for the re-key.  The
-- arm-discriminating coverage lives in `i4-xmod-requires-foreign-iface-rejected/`.
-- A fixture whose stated purpose cannot fire reads as coverage while providing
-- none, which is why no such purpose is claimed here.
--
-- OBSERVED SITE, recorded because it differs from the obvious reading and a later
-- reader should not have to re-derive it: the diagnostic lands at `lib.mdk:25`
-- (`roundtrip`'s own body), NOT at `main`'s call.  `roundtrip`'s `a` escapes its
-- generalized group anchored to nothing, so the verdict fires while `lib` is being
-- checked — which is the arm where both sides carry `mod:lib`.  The entry file is
-- still load-bearing: it is what makes `lib` a MODULE rather than a flat program, and
-- deleting it collapses the fixture to the `TkBare`/`TkBare` shape the four existing
-- single-file ambiguity fixtures already cover.
--
-- `lib.mdk` holds the interface, its two concrete-head instances, and the
-- constrained producer whose type fixes `a` nowhere; this file only imports and
-- calls it.
import lib.{Mk, roundtrip}

main = println (roundtrip 14)
